{"qid":"Q705904","names":["Seleucid Empire"],"title":"Seleucid Empire","endonym":{"native":"Σελευκίδαι","lang":"grc"},"capsule":"The Seleucid Empire was founded by Seleucus I Nicator, one of Alexander the Great's generals, who secured Babylon in 312 BC — the epoch year of the Seleucid era — and, after his victory at Ipsus in 301 BC, added Syria and eastern Asia Minor, eventually ruling a realm stretching from Thrace through Persia to the borders of India. At its height under Antiochus III 'the Great' (r. 222–187 BC) — who campaigned as far as Bactria and India in the anabasis of 212–205 BC and briefly recovered Syria and Coele-Syria — the empire was the largest territorial state of the Hellenistic world. Antiochus III's defeat by Rome at the Battle of Magnesia (190 BC) forced cession of Asia Minor under the Treaty of Apamea (188 BC), triggering irreversible decline; the Maccabean revolt (167 BC), Parthian seizure of Iran, and dynastic civil wars reduced the state to a Syrian rump that Pompey absorbed into the Roman province of Syria in 63 BC.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Seleucid core territories in Syria, Mesopotamia, and western Iran are attested by cuneiform administrative tablets, Greek inscriptions, and coin evidence; eastern frontier zones in Bactria and India are reconstructed principally from literary sources and numismatic evidence, with significant scholarly debate over extent and timing of losses.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Seleucid empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Seleucid-Empire"},{"label":"Livius.org: Seleucids","url":"https://www.livius.org/articles/dynasty/seleucids/"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Seleucid dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Seleucus I Nicator","nativeName":"Σέλευκος Α΄ Νικάτωρ","roman":"Seleukos I Nikatōr","start":-312,"end":-281,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Antiochus I Soter","nativeName":"Ἀντίοχος Α΄ Σωτήρ","roman":"Antiochos I Sōtēr","start":-281,"end":-261,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Antiochus II Theos","nativeName":"Ἀντίοχος Β΄ Θεός","roman":"Antiochos II Theos","start":-261,"end":-246,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Seleucus II Callinicus","nativeName":"Σέλευκος Β΄ Καλλίνικος","roman":"Seleukos II Kallinikos","start":-246,"end":-225,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Seleucus III Ceraunus","nativeName":"Σέλευκος Γ΄ Κεραυνός","roman":"Seleukos III Keraunos","start":-225,"end":-223,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Antiochus III the Great","nativeName":"Ἀντίοχος Γ΄ Μέγας","roman":"Antiochos III Megas","start":-223,"end":-187,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Seleucus IV Philopator","nativeName":"Σέλευκος Δ΄ Φιλοπάτωρ","roman":"Seleukos IV Philopatōr","start":-187,"end":-175,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Antiochus IV Epiphanes","nativeName":"Ἀντίοχος Δ΄ Ἐπιφανής","roman":"Antiochos IV Epiphanēs","start":-175,"end":-164,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Antiochus V Eupator","nativeName":"Ἀντίοχος Ε΄ Εὐπάτωρ","roman":"Antiochos V Eupatōr","start":-164,"end":-162,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Demetrius I Soter","nativeName":"Δημήτριος Α΄ Σωτήρ","roman":"Dēmētrios I Sōtēr","start":-162,"end":-150,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Alexander I Balas","nativeName":"Ἀλέξανδρος Α΄ Βάλας","roman":"Alexand